// VS Code keybindings — shared across Linux and Windows.
// Docs: https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/getstarted/keybindings
// Defaults for reference: Ctrl+K Ctrl+S opens the keybindings UI, and its
// "{}" button (top right) shows the full default list as JSON.
//
// Linked to:  Linux ~/.config/Code/User/keybindings.json
//             Windows %APPDATA%\Code\User\keybindings.json
//
// This file only ADDS to VS Code's built-in bindings; it doesn't replace them,
// and a binding only fires when its `when` clause is true.
//
// Almost everything keyboard-related in this repo is a VIM mapping, which lives
// in settings.json under "vim.normalModeKeyBindingsNonRecursive" — that's the
// file to edit for editing/navigation keys. This file is for the two kinds of
// chord a Vim mapping can't express:
//
//   1. Chords that must work OUTSIDE a Vim buffer (the Markdown preview below).
//   2. ALT chords. VSCodeVim only receives the keys it registers in its own
//      package.json — 75 of them, and not one is alt+<letter>. So an `<A-j>` in
//      vim.normalModeKeyBindings never fires: VS Code routes alt+j to its own
//      keybinding table and the Vim extension never sees it. Alt maps belong here.
//
// User keybindings in this file BEAT extension-contributed ones, which matters
// for the alt+k collision documented below.
[
  {
    // --- Markdown: make Ctrl+Shift+V a real toggle ---
    // settings.json opens .md files in the rendered preview by default, so the
    // way back to the source needs a key. VS Code's default Ctrl+Shift+V only
    // goes one way (source -> preview, `markdown.showPreview`); this adds the
    // return trip, so the same chord flips between rendered and source.
    //
    // `markdownPreviewFocus` is true only while the preview pane itself has
    // focus, so this never shadows the default binding in a text buffer. And
    // because the preview isn't an editable buffer, Vim mode isn't involved —
    // which is exactly why this can't live in settings.json.
    "key": "ctrl+shift+v",
    "command": "markdown.showSource",
    "when": "markdownPreviewFocus"
  },

  // --- Move the current line up/down ---
  // .ideavimrc maps <A-k>/<A-j> to MoveLineUp/MoveLineDown; VS Code ships this on
  // Alt+Up/Down only. These add the Vim-letter spelling, so the same keys move a
  // line in JetBrains and here. Not scoped to a Vim mode on purpose — in
  // JetBrains it works while typing too.
  {
    "key": "alt+k",
    "command": "editor.action.moveLinesUpAction",
    "when": "editorTextFocus && !editorReadonly"
  },
  {
    "key": "alt+j",
    "command": "editor.action.moveLinesDownAction",
    "when": "editorTextFocus && !editorReadonly"
  },

  // --- Claude Code: insert an @-mention file reference ---
  // COLLISION, and why this binding exists: the Claude Code extension claims
  // plain `alt+k` for this command, which the alt+k above now wins (user
  // keybindings beat extension ones). Rather than lose the feature, it moves one
  // key over. Its other chords are unaffected — though the extension's
  // Cmd+Escape "focus" binding is the Windows key on Windows, so use <leader>a
  // (settings.json) to open Claude instead.
  {
    "key": "alt+shift+k",
    "command": "claude-vscode.insertAtMention",
    "when": "editorTextFocus"
  }
]
